<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Privacy manifest for SentryMobile (Sentry for iPhone).

  App Store Connect rejects an iOS upload outright when a bundle that uses a
  "required reason" API ships without one of these, so this file is an upload
  gate rather than a review nicety. Every entry below was derived by reading
  the code that actually compiles into this bundle — this target's own sources
  plus the embedded SentryKit.framework (project.yml's `SentryKit_iOS`,
  which compiles the whole SentryKit directory, not the trimmed subset the
  watchOS variant takes). Nothing here is copied from a template; a wrong
  reason code is a rejection, so each one names its evidence.

  Reason-code text quoted below comes from Apple's
  "NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons" documentation:
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/app-privacy-configuration/nsprivacyaccessedapitypes/nsprivacyaccessedapitypereasons
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Not a tracking app, and there is nothing to qualify. Plan §14.3 states
	  the commitment this key encodes: no telemetry, no analytics, no
	  third-party crash reporting, no advertising SDKs, and no network access
	  at all beyond the user's own Mac. There is no third-party SDK in this
	  bundle that could track on our behalf either — the only package this
	  app links is GRDB (SQLite), which ships its own manifest declaring no
	  collection.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  Empty because this app contacts no internet host whatsoever. Its only
	  transport is `LocalSyncClient` (SentryKit/LocalSync/LocalSyncClient.swift):
	  a Bonjour-discovered TCP connection to a Mac on the same Wi-Fi, or a
	  user-typed host:port dialed with TLS-PSK. Both are addresses the user
	  supplied or that their own Mac advertised — no domain we control, and
	  nothing to list here.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<!--
	  Empty, deliberately. This entry once declared
	  NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation for the "Location Log"
	  feature (a Mac-collected coordinate this app displayed on a map). That
	  feature was deleted outright — no CoreLocation call remains anywhere in
	  this bundle or in the SentryKit framework it embeds, `SystemSnapshot`
	  carries no location field, and nothing in this app handles a
	  coordinate. Everything else the app displays (battery, CPU, memory,
	  thermal readings from the user's own Mac) travels between two of the
	  user's own devices over their own network, is never sent to any server,
	  and is not accessible to the developer — which is not "collection"
	  under Apple's definition.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<!--
			  CA92.1 — "Declare this reason to access user defaults to read
			  and write information that is only accessible to the app
			  itself."
			  Evidence: the `@AppStorage` properties in
			  SentryMobile/RootTabView.swift ("selectedThemeID") and
			  SentryMobile/Settings/SettingsTabView.swift ("selectedThemeID",
			  "remoteSync.host", "remoteSync.port", "remoteSync.code",
			  "liveActivityEnabled"), and `UserDefaults.standard` in
			  SentryMobile/Data/AppDataSource.swift's
			  `remoteEndpointFromDefaults()`, which reads those same four
			  remote-sync keys back, and in
			  SentryMobile/LiveActivity/KeepAwakeActivityController.swift,
			  which reads "liveActivityEnabled" back from outside a `View`
			  (it is not a `View` and so has no `@AppStorage` of its own —
			  see `enabledDefaultsKey`). All of it is this app's own standard
			  suite; nothing reads a key written by another app or the
			  system.

			  1C8F.1 — "Declare this reason to access user defaults to read
			  and write information only accessible to the apps, app
			  extensions, and App Clips that are members of the same App
			  Group as the app itself."
			  Evidence: `WidgetSnapshotStore`
			  (SentryKit/Sync/WidgetSnapshot.swift) and `WatchRelayStore`
			  (SentryKit/Watch/WatchRelayStore.swift) both open
			  `UserDefaults(suiteName: "group.dev.malekswilam.sentry")`,
			  which is exactly the group in
			  SentryMobile/SentryMobile.entitlements. That suite is how the
			  app hands a snapshot to its widget extension, which runs in a
			  separate process that may not be alive when the snapshot
			  arrives.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<!--
			  35F9.1 — "Declare this reason to access the system boot time in
			  order to measure the amount of time that has elapsed between
			  events that occurred within the app or to perform calculation to
			  enable timers. Information accessed for this reason, or any
			  derived information, may not be sent off-device. There is an
			  exception that allows the app to send information about the
			  amount of time that has elapsed between events that occurred
			  within the app off-device."

			  Evidence: `ProcessInfo.processInfo.systemUptime` in
			  SentryKit/Insights/DeviceFacts.swift (`DeviceFacts.current()`),
			  which compiles into the SentryKit.framework embedded in this
			  app because `SentryKit_iOS` takes the whole directory.

			  What it is used for: "how long since the last restart," fed into
			  `InsightContext` so a rule can say something specific about this
			  machine. The only caller is `InsightContextBuilder`, and the
			  only caller of *that* is Sentry/Insights/InsightsViewModel.swift
			  — the Mac app. On iPhone the code is present in the binary but
			  never executed, so the boot time is not merely kept on-device,
			  it is never read at all. It is also deliberately excluded from
			  the snapshot that goes over the wire: see the
			  `case .systemUptimeSeconds: return nil` comment in
			  SentryKit/Models/SystemSnapshot+MetricValue.swift.
			-->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		  Deliberately absent, having been searched for rather than assumed:

		  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace — nothing in this bundle calls
		  `volumeAvailableCapacity*`, `statfs`, or
		  `attributesOfFileSystem(forPath:)`. The disk figures the app shows
		  are read on the *Mac* by SystemMetricsKit, which is a macOS-only
		  target and is not part of this bundle. (Had they been read here,
		  85F4.1 would be the reason — it explicitly permits sending disk
		  space to another device of the same person over the local network.)

		  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp — no `creationDate`,
		  `contentModificationDate`, `attributesOfItem(atPath:)` or
		  `URLResourceValues` timestamp read anywhere in this target or in
		  SentryKit. GRDB is the one third-party package linked here and it
		  ships its own PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy.

		  NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryActiveKeyboards — no
		  `UITextInputMode.activeInputModes` anywhere; this app has no custom
		  keyboard and no keyboard-dependent UI.
		-->
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
